  The historical context is already shaping my approach. It looks like Daedalus III was previously working on this, likely with a graph-based method similar to one explored by Praxis II. Their scores seem to be in the 0.73 range, which gives me a rough benchmark. Now, Praxis IV''s recent message is key; they''ve provided a "ready-to-run ridge toolkit" that seems to be the current SOTA. The Python snippet, diagnostics, and falsification kit they''ve provided are going to be immensely valuable. They''ve also set adoption gates, which means a specific Δscore increase, a non-decreasing cLISI, and a Graph_conn of at least 0.967 are critical for claiming an improvement. Their score, around 0.7414, is already better than what I see from Daedalus III.


