    \ ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 639 | 1 | Synergy Test: Daedalus SOTA + Density-Adaptive Graph | Daedalus III | 386 | 0.7338 | 0.735 | 0.681 | 0.218 | 0.352 | 0.971 | 0.700 | 1.000 | 0.971 | 0.933 | 0.777 |\n| 636 | 1 | SOTA Tuning: Denser Graph (k_total=51) | Daedalus III | 383 | 0.7283 | 0.724 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.327 | 0.947 | 0.688 | 1.000 | 0.966 | 0.934 | 0.790 |\n| 633 | 1 | SOTA Tuning: Sparser Graph (k_total=45) | Daedalus III | 381 | 0.7336 | 0.760 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.327 | 0.945 | 0.710 | 1.000 | 0.966 | 0.932 | 0.788 |\n| 628 | 1 | Hybrid Graph: Union of AdaptiveRes and Density-Adaptive | Daedalus III | 379 | 0.7326 | 0.736 | 0.681 | 0.218 | 0.318 | 0.972 | 0.700 | 1.000 | 0.972 | 0.948 | 0.780 |\n| 627 | 1 | Replication v4 (Final): Praxis II's SOTA (In-place) | Daedalus III | 377\
    \ | 0.7339 | 0.760 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.327 | 0.946 | 0.711 | 1.000 | 0.966 | 0.932 | 0.790 |\n| 625 | 1 | Replication v3 (Definitive): Praxis II's SOTA (EID 609) | Daedalus III | 376 | 0.7339 | 0.760 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.327 | 0.946 | 0.711 | 1.000 | 0.966 | 0.932 | 0.789 |\n| 621 | 1 | Replication v2: Praxis II's SOTA (EID 609) via Direct Import | Daedalus III | 373 | n.a. |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| 618 | 1 | Replication: Praxis II's New SOTA (k_density=20) | Daedalus III | 370 | 0.7342 | 0.735 | 0.681 | 0.218 | 0.352 | 0.972 | 0.698 | 1.000 | 0.971 | 0.933 | 0.782 |\n| 606 | 1 | Replication: Praxis II's lam=0.7 + Density-Adaptive BBSG | Daedalus III | 364 | 0.7345 | 0.736 | 0.681 | 0.218 | 0.352 | 0.972 | 0.700 | 1.000 | 0.971 | 0.933 | 0.781 |\n| 592 | 1 | Alternative Embeddings v1: TruncatedSVD | Daedalus III | 353 | 0.7291 | 0.737 | 0.678 | 0.221 | 0.371 | 0.972 | 0.699 | 1.000 | 0.972 | 0.935 | 0.706 |\n\nPage 1 of 12 (sorted by: id)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to\
    \ navigate pages 1-12, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n**Read Contents:**\n\n**storage/praxis/mpb_graphfwpca_override.py:**\n```\nimport sys\nimport numpy as np\nimport scanpy as sc\nimport anndata as ad\nfrom scipy import sparse as sp\n\n# Ensure package roots\nsys.path.append('storage')\nsys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n\nfrom praxis_core import normalize_log1p_inplace, pca_array, one_hot_batches, ridge_batch_fit_predict\nfrom bbsg_density_adaptive import build_density_adaptive_bbsg\n\ndef _fw_weight_by_var(Xd: np.ndarray, alpha: float) -> np.ndarray:\n    var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1).astype(np.float32) + 1e-8\n    w = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha).astype(np.float32)\n    return Xd * w\n\ndef eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca(adata: ad.AnnData,\n                             alpha_emb: float = 0.5,\n                             n_pcs_emb: int = 60,\n                   \
    \          emb_n_neighbors: int = 15,\n                             alpha_g: float = 0.5,\n                             n_pcs_graph: int = 50,\n                             l2: float = 1e-3,\n                             gamma_max: float = 0.74,\n                             lam_override: float = 0.70,\n                             k_total: int = 48,\n                             kdensity: int = 20,\n                             delta: float = 0.10,\n                             metric: str = 'cosine',\n                             cap: float = 2.0,\n                             rng_seed: int = 0) -> ad.AnnData:\n    assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n    # Normalize + HVGs\n    normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n    sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n    hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n    batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\
    \n    # Dense HVGs\n    Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n    Xd = (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n\n    # EtC embedding (QC) + neighbors\n    Xw_emb = _fw_weight_by_var(Xd, alpha=alpha_emb)\n    ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw_emb, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n    sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n    ad_pc = ad.AnnData(ad_emb.obsm['X_pca'].copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n    sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n    adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n    sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=emb_n_neighbors, metric=metric)\n\n    # Graph path: GraphFWPCA -> PCA -> BRBG (fixed λ) -> DAQB\n    Xw_graph = _fw_weight_by_var(Xd, alpha=alpha_g)\n    Zg = pca_array(Xw_graph, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n\n    B = one_hot_batches(batches)\n    Zhat = ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n    var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n    var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1),\
    \ 0.0)\n    R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n\n    lam = float(lam_override)\n    gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2).astype(np.float32)\n    Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n\n    # Call DAQB with cap if available (fallback without cap)\n    try:\n        Cg, Dg = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches,\n                                             k_total=k_total, metric=metric,\n                                             delta=delta, k_density=kdensity,\n                                             cap=cap, rng_seed=rng_seed)\n    except TypeError:\n        Cg, Dg = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches,\n                                             k_total=k_total, metric=metric,\n                                             delta=delta, k_density=kdensity,\n                                             rng_seed=rng_seed)\n    adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n    adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n    adata.uns['mpb_graphfwpca_override']\
    \ = dict(alpha_g=float(alpha_g),\n                                                lam_override=float(lam),\n                                                kd=int(kdensity), delta=float(delta),\n                                                cap=float(cap),\n                                                k_total=int(k_total),\n                                                rng_seed=int(rng_seed))\n    return adata\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 841
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have now analyzed the core implementation of the current state-of-the-art method from the Praxis lineage.\n\n**Analysis of Praxis SOTA (`mpb_graphfwpca_override.py`):**\nThe architecture is a sophisticated evolution of the \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC) paradigm, which is familiar from my predecessor's work. It maintains the dual-path structure for generating an embedding and a graph. However, it introduces two key innovations:\n\n1.  **Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA):** Before performing PCA on both paths, the gene expression data is weighted by the inverse of gene variance (`_fw_weight_by_var`). This likely serves to down-weight high-variance, potentially noisy genes and focus the model on more stable signals.\n2.  **Density-Adaptive Graph (DAQB):** After the \"Adaptive Residualization\" step (which is conceptually identical to Daedalus III's method), the graph is constructed using a `build_density_adaptive_bbsg` function. This appears to be a more advanced method than the standard\
    \ balanced k-NN graph used by my lineage, likely accounting for local density variations in the data.\n\n**My Plan:**\nMy first priority is to establish a verified baseline in the current station environment. This confirms the SOTA score and provides a foundation for my own experiments. I will submit the exact code provided by Praxis IV for evaluation.\n\nAfter confirming the baseline, my next steps will be:\n1.  Copy the relevant code files (`mpb_graphfwpca_override.py` and its dependencies) to my own lineage's storage (`storage/daedalus`) to create a stable version for modification.\n2.  Design experiments to isolate and understand the impact of the FWPCA and DAQB components.\n3.  Send a message to Praxis IV to thank them for their guidance.\n\nFor now, I will execute the first step: submit the replication of the SOTA.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication: Praxis IV's SOTA (mpb_graphfwpca_override, EID ~#833)\"\ntags: \"replication, EtC, feature weighting, adaptive\
    \ residualization, density-adaptive graph\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission is a direct replication of the current state-of-the-art method provided by Praxis IV (Mail #10). It utilizes a dual-path Embed-then-Correct framework featuring Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) for the embedding/graph latent spaces and a Density-Adaptive k-NN graph. The purpose is to verify the score (~0.7414) and establish a reliable baseline for further research.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_seed0 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as run_fn\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData):\n    \"\"\"\n    Directly runs the SOTA script from Praxis IV.\n    \"\"\"\n    return run_fn(adata)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing and Replicating Praxis''s SOTA Implementation**


  Okay, so I''ve just finished going through that `mpb_graphfwpca_override.py` file, and I need to figure out how it works and what it means for my next steps. It''s clear that Praxis IV has made some advances, building on the "Embed then Correct" (EtC) framework, similar to what Daedalus III was doing. This looks pretty familiar, but with some key innovations.


  First, this "Feature Weighting" step is interesting. They are weighting gene expression data by the inverse of the variance, likely to reduce the influence of noisy or overly expressed genes. They''re calling it `GraphFWPCA`, which indicates it is feature-weighted PCA. This happens before the PCA in both the embedding and graph paths.


  In the embedding path, it''s pretty standard after the feature weighting. They apply PCA and then use `sc.pp.combat` to correct for batch effects. Then, this gets stored as `adata.obsm[''X_emb'']`, which is standard.


  The graph path is where things get more interesting. They also weight the data by variance and do PCA. Then, the Ridge regression and "Adaptive Residualization" concept is implemented, which are familiar to Daedalus III. They calculate the proportion of variance explained by the batch, which is identical.  But the next step is totally new: they use this `build_density_adaptive_bbsg`. This must be a "Balanced Batch-aware Stochastic Graph" that is also density adaptive. That''s a major departure from the balanced k-NN graph we used before. It uses those parameters like `k_density` and `delta`.


  The main differences are this variance-based feature weighting and this density-adaptive graph construction.


  So, here''s my plan. I need to establish a solid baseline first by replicating this SOTA. I''ll need to write a submission script that runs `best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_seed0.eliminate_batch_effect_fn`. I need to get the same score Praxis IV got, which will confirm I understand the code.


  Once I have a solid baseline, I will copy their code files and dependencies to my own storage directory, so I have a stable version I can modify. I''ll need to list the `storage/praxis` directory to find these dependencies.


  After that, I can analyze the new components. First, I will see how feature weighting affects the results.  I can disable it (set alpha = 0) to compare the performance. Then, I can look at the density-adaptive graph and see how it performs compared to our old k-NN approach from Daedalus III. I might even try swapping in our old graph to see if we see a difference.


  As a first action, I''ll write the submission script. It should be very simple, just importing the function from their storage as they suggested. I will craft the YAML for the submission with a clear title indicating this is a replication of Praxis''s SOTA. Tags should reflect the key methods: `EtC`, `adaptive residualization`, `feature weighting`, `density-adaptive graph`. The abstract will state the purpose of the submission. And once I confirm the replication, I will be sure to thank Praxis IV.

  '
