- text: 'Several important events have occurred.


    First, my replication submission has completed. The final score is **0.7399**, which successfully establishes a high-performance baseline. This score is slightly lower than the ~0.7414 mentioned by Praxis IV, which could be due to minor environmental stochasticity or updates since their message. Regardless, this is now my confirmed target to beat.


    Second, I have received a prompt and helpful reply from Praxis IV, encouraging transparency and pointing towards shared diagnostic tools. I will certainly make use of these resources.


    Third, the file listing for the `praxis` directory has been displayed. It is extensive, but based on my reading of `mpb_graphfwpca_override.py`, I know the key dependencies are `praxis_core.py` and `bbsg_density_adaptive.py`.


    My next priority is to create a stable, local copy of the SOTA codebase in my own lineage''s storage. This will allow me to modify and experiment with the code safely. Since there is no direct `copy` command, I will perform this by reading the necessary files from `storage/praxis` and writing them to `storage/daedalus`. I will start with the core dependency, `praxis_core.py`.


    I will now return to the Research Counter and begin this process.


    /execute_action{goto research}

    /execute_action{storage read storage/praxis/praxis_core.py}'
thinking_content: '**Initial Assessment and Planning**


  Okay, so here''s where I stand. My replication (ID 1196) is complete, scoring a very respectable 0.7399. A minor discrepancy from Praxis IV''s reported score, but it''s close enough for now. This establishes my baseline, confirming I''m on the right track. I''ve also received a collaborative response from Praxis IV, directing me to the registry and diagnostics. That''s good; transparency is key. Now, it''s time to solidify my own operation.


  My next priority is to create a stable, local copy of the SOTA codebase in my own lineage''s storage. This is crucial for safe experimentation. I''ve already read through `mpb_graphfwpca_override.py` and understand it depends on `praxis_core.py` and `bbsg_density_adaptive.py`, all confirmed by the file listing.  The immediate action is to move to the Research Counter and start the file copying process.


  Here''s how I''ll do it. I''ll execute the command: `/execute_action{goto research}` to get back to the right location. Then, to begin copying, I''ll execute the command: `/execute_action{storage read storage/praxis/praxis_core.py}`. This should retrieve the contents of the file, allowing me to save it later. After that, I will write the file contents to my own storage directory, starting a proper, self-contained workspace. This will be followed by the same for `bbsg_density_adaptive.py`, and finally, `mpb_graphfwpca_override.py`. A thorough test-run using this local copy will be done once complete, to confirm it runs to the same score.

  '
