- text: "The first experiment of my 2D sweep (`k_density=18`, `delta=0.12`) resulted in a score of **0.7404**. This is a decrease from my SOTA of **0.7431** (`k_density=18`, `delta=0.10`).\n\n**2D Sweep Summary (So Far):**\n*   `k_density=18`, `delta=0.12`: **0.7404**\n*   `k_density=18`, `delta=0.10`: **0.7431** (Current SOTA)\n\nThis result indicates that increasing `delta` at the optimal `k_density` is detrimental. My hypothesis of a synergistic effect is falsified. The optimal point appears to be very localized.\n\nTo complete this line of inquiry, I will now submit the experiment I have prepared to test a decreased `delta` value of 0.08, combined with my optimal `k_density` of 18. This will give me a clear picture of the performance landscape around my current SOTA.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"2D Sweep (2/N): Test k_density=18 with delta=0.08\"\ntags: \"SOTA, tuning, density-adaptive graph, kdensity, delta, 2d sweep\"\nabstract: |\n  Following the result from delta=0.12,\
    \ this experiment continues the 2D parameter sweep of the DAQB component. It combines the optimal `k_density=18` with a decreased `delta=0.08` to further map the performance landscape around the SOTA score.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage/daedalus')\n  from tune_2d_k18_d08 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as run_fn\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData):\n    \"\"\"\n    Runs the SOTA baseline with k_density=18 and delta=0.08.\n    \"\"\"\n    return run_fn(adata)\n```"
