tick: 900
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 900\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Daedalus IV\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Daedalus legacy by applying methodical analysis to achieve novel research breakthroughs.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 175,029 / 300,000 used (58%)\n- Agent Age: 72 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #37: Batch-Robust Feature Selection Improves Performance of Simple Baselines for Single-Cell Batch Integration**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Axiom II, Created at Tick: 898\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We introduce 'Batch-Robust Feature Selection', a preprocessing method for scRNA-seq batch integration. The method identifies a consensus set of highly variable genes (HVGs) by taking the intersection of HVGs found in each batch independently. We demonstrate its generalizability by applying it to two different baselines: a linear correction model (Combat) and a simple graph-based model (PCA+kNN).\
    \ In both cases, our method significantly improved biological conservation (ARI score) and the overall integration score, demonstrating its effectiveness as a simple, interpretable, and generalizable feature selection strategy.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_37-1**\nAuthor: Axiom II (Tick 898)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Batch-Robust Feature Selection Improves Performance of Simple Baselines for Single-Cell Batch Integration\n\n---\n\nContent:\n### 1. Introduction\n\nBatch correction is a critical step in single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) analysis. The performance of common integration methods is often highly dependent on the initial set of highly variable genes (HVGs) used for analysis. Standard HVG selection may inadvertently select genes driven by technical batch effects rather than stable biological signals.\n\nIn this paper, we introduce \"Batch-Robust Feature Selection,\" a simple and interpretable preprocessing method. We hypothesize that by performing\
    \ analysis on a consensus set of genes that are consistently highly variable across all batches, the performance of simple baselines can be improved. We demonstrate this method's efficacy and generalizability by applying it to two different baselines—a linear correction method (Combat) and a simple graph-based method (PCA+kNN)—and contrast it with an alternative approach that fails.\n\n### 2. Methods\n\n**2.1 Batch-Robust Feature Selection Algorithm**\nOur primary method identifies a consensus set of HVGs.\n1.  Data is normalized and log-transformed.\n2.  For each of the B batches, the top 2000 HVGs are identified independently (`flavor='cell_ranger'`, with a `seurat` fallback).\n3.  The \"batch-robust\" feature set is the intersection of these B sets (1,578 genes for the Human Heart dataset).\n4.  The chosen baseline method is then applied to the data subsetted to this robust feature set.\n\n**2.2 Alternative Method Tested: Contextual Combat**\nAs a negative control, we tested an alternative\
    \ hypothesis: making Combat's correction more granular. This method involved performing a rough initial correction, using unsupervised clustering to find 'meta-clusters', and then applying Combat to the intersection of original batches and these meta-clusters.\n\n**2.3 Experimental Design**\nWe conducted five experiments in total: replications of two baselines (Combat, PCA+kNN), our proposed method applied to both, and the 'Contextual Combat' negative control.\n\n### 3. Results\n\nOur proposed method demonstrated a significant performance improvement across both baselines, while the alternative 'Contextual Combat' method degraded performance.\n\nThe table below presents the full 10-metric vectors for all five experiments. Metrics are in the standard order: [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle].\n\n| Experiment                       | ID   | Score  | ASW_b | ASW_l | ARI   | NMI   | G_conn| kBET  | iLISI | cLISI | PCR   | C_cycle |\n|----------------------------------|------|--------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|---------|\n\
    | Combat Baseline                  | 1215 | 0.5896 | 0.664 | 0.223 | 0.493 | 0.683 | 0.885 | 0.604 | 0.033 | 0.988 | 1.000 | 0.323   |\n| **+ Batch-Robust Features**      | 1224 | 0.6033 | 0.671 | 0.224 | **0.608** | **0.708** | 0.883 | 0.597 | 0.047 | 0.988 | 1.000 | 0.308   |\n| Contextual Combat (Negative Ctrl)| 1220 | 0.5649 | 0.821 | 0.148 | 0.299 | 0.451 | 0.894 | 0.941 | 0.316 | 0.727 | 1.000 | 0.052   |\n| PCA+kNN Baseline                 | 1247 | 0.4546 | 0.516 | 0.208 | 0.415 | 0.649 | 0.890 | 0.527 | 0.003 | 0.991 | 0.000 | 0.348   |\n| **+ Batch-Robust Features**      | 1250 | 0.5136 | 0.582 | 0.225 | **0.704** | **0.711** | 0.902 | 0.642 | 0.063 | 0.990 | 0.000 | 0.319   |\n\n### 4. Discussion\n\nThe success of \"Batch-Robust Feature Selection\" across two different baselines validates our hypothesis that a consensus feature set provides a more stable and biologically informative input for integration algorithms. This was particularly evident in the strong gains in biological\
    \ conservation (ARI). In contrast, the 'Contextual Combat' experiment serves as a valuable negative result, demonstrating that granular correction can easily lead to over-correction and loss of biological signal.\n\nOur primary finding aligns with a broader theme in the station's research (e.g., Praxis II, Archive #28) that highlights the critical importance of input feature engineering. We contribute a simple, interpretable, and generalizable hard-selection method that effectively improves the performance of simple baselines.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_37-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 899)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nAccept. This is a solid submission that fully meets all publication criteria after a successful revision. The paper presents a simple, well-motivated feature selection method and rigorously validates it. The inclusion of a generalization experiment, demonstrating the method's effectiveness\
    \ across two fundamentally different baselines (linear correction and graph-based), is a major strength and a model of good scientific practice. The paper is well-written, the claims are well-supported, and it makes a clear and valuable contribution to the understanding of how to improve simple integration methods.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is a well-executed study. A valuable next step would be to investigate the synergy, or lack thereof, between your 'Batch-Robust Feature Selection' and the more complex SOTA pipeline. The SOTA method uses a soft-weighting scheme (FWPCA) for its features. Does applying your hard-selection method beforehand provide any additional benefit, or is its effect made redundant by the SOTA's more sophisticated feature engineering? Answering this question would provide critical insight into the relationship between hard- and soft-selection methods in advanced integration pipelines.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system\
    \ to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Content Update Notification**\n\nA archive room item you previously read has been updated:\n**Batch-Robust Feature Selection Improves Performance of Simple Baselines for Single-Cell Batch Integration** (Item: archive_37-1)\n\nYour read status has been cleared for this content. To view the updated content, use: `/execute_action{read archive_37-1}` in the Archive Room.\n### Message 3\n\nYour research submission 'Tune DAQB: Decrease delta to 0.06' (ID: 1256) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.7429642972078824\n**ASW_batch:** 0.659\n**ASW_label:** 0.248\n**ARI:** 0.775\n**NMI:** 0.729\n**Graph_conn:** 0.967\n**kBET:** 0.788\n**iLISI:** 0.963\n**cLISI:** 0.973\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.327\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.659  ASW_label: 0.248  ARI: 0.775  NMI: 0.729\n  Graph_conn: 0.967  kBET: 0.788  iLISI: 0.963  cLISI: 0.973\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle:\
