tick: 902
role: model
parts:
- text: "My synergy test experiment is running. This is a pivotal experiment that combines insights from two different lineages.\n\nWhile waiting for the results, I will prepare a contingency experiment. My exploration of the SOTA parameters has been successful but seems to be reaching a point of diminishing returns. The gains from tuning `delta` were small, and my initial probe of `alpha` was negative. It is important to continue exploring fundamentally different ideas.\n\nPraxis IV recently ran several experiments with \"Anscombe\" variants (IDs 1258, 1259, 1260). Anscombe transformation is a variance-stabilizing transformation, often used for data with Poisson-like distributions, such as UMI counts in scRNA-seq. This is a promising idea that alters the very first step of the data preprocessing.\n\nI will prepare a script to test the Anscombe transformation on my current SOTA model. This will be my next experiment if the synergy test is not a major breakthrough.\n\n/execute_action{storage\
    \ write daedalus/idea_anscombe.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import numpy as np\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  sys.path.append('storage/daedalus')\n  from mpb_graphfwpca_override import eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca\n\n  def anscombe_transform_inplace(adata):\n      \"\"\"Applies Anscombe transform to adata.X.\"\"\"\n      print(\"Applying Anscombe transformation...\")\n      if 'counts' not in adata.layers:\n          adata.layers['counts'] = adata.X.copy()\n      \n      # Using sparse-safe operations if needed\n      if isinstance(adata.X, sc.sparse.csr_matrix) or isinstance(adata.X, sc.sparse.csc_matrix):\n          adata.X.data = 2 * np.sqrt(adata.X.data + 3/8)\n      else:\n          adata.X = 2 * np.sqrt(adata.X + 3/8)\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Tests Anscombe transformation as a preprocessing step for the SOTA model.\n      Replaces normalize_total and log1p.\n\
    \      \"\"\"\n      print(\"[daedalus_anscombe_test] Anscombe transform + SOTA\")\n      \n      # The standard SOTA pipeline's normalize_log1p_inplace is skipped.\n      # Instead, we apply Anscombe transform directly on raw counts.\n      # The mpb_graphfwpca_override function has its own normalization call,\n      # so we must modify the input data *before* calling it and ensure it\n      # doesn't re-normalize.\n      \n      # We will create a modified version of the main function that skips normalization.\n      # For now, this script is a placeholder to be refined.\n      # The logic below is a simplified concept.\n      \n      anscombe_transform_inplace(adata)\n      \n      # This needs a modified version of eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca that skips its internal normalization.\n      # For now, this is a conceptual placeholder. I will create the full script later.\n      return eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca(\n          adata,\n          lam_override=0.605,\n          delta=0.08,\n  \
    \        kdensity=18,\n          k_total=50,\n          rng_seed=0\n      )\n```\n*Self-correction:* I realize while writing this that simply applying the transformation is not enough, as the downstream function `eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca` performs its own `normalize_log1p_inplace` step, which would overwrite my transformation. To properly test this, I will need to create a new version of that core function that omits the normalization step. I will do this after my current experiment completes. The current file `idea_anscombe.py` serves as a good starting point for the transformation logic itself."
