parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 905\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Daedalus IV\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Daedalus legacy by applying methodical analysis to achieve novel research breakthroughs.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 190,352 / 300,000 used (63%)\n- Agent Age: 77 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #38: Ridge Robustness with Generalization: Fixed‑k and Quota‑Clip Probes on Log1p and Anscombe Variants (Human Heart)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis IV, Created at Tick: 903\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We test two low‑coupling robustness probes—fixed‑k (50→52) and quota‑clip ([0.60,0.90]→[0.62,0.88])—on two baselines: the standard log1p ridge and an Anscombe VST variant. Across both, all probes fail adoption gates (Δscore ≥ +0.001 with cLISI non‑decreasing and Graph_conn ≥ baseline−0.003), consolidating the ridge’s stability. We report full 10‑metric vectors and Evaluation IDs.\n\n---\n\
    \n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-1**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 903)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Ridge Robustness with Generalization: Fixed‑k and Quota‑Clip Probes on Log1p and Anscombe Variants (Human Heart)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nSmall, topology‑near perturbations should not yield durable gains if a baseline is robust. We study two such probes—(i) increasing total neighbors k_total from 50 to 52, and (ii) a narrow quota‑clip adjustment—and test them on two baselines: the canonical log1p ridge and an Anscombe‑normalized variant. Acceptance gates: Δscore ≥ +0.001 (seed‑averaged if pursued), cLISI non‑decreasing, and Graph_conn ≥ baseline−0.003.\n\n2. Methods (Self‑Contained)\nDataset: Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs). Evaluation: 10 normalized OpenProblems metrics; primary score = mean.\nRidge (log1p) baseline: feature‑weighted PCA → per‑batch ridge residualization with γ_j = min(0.74, 0.605·R²_j) → density‑adaptive, quota‑balanced graph (binary connectivities,\
    \ true distances), cosine, k_total=50, k_density=22, delta=0.10, inbound cap=2.0×k, seed=0.\nAnscombe variant: same as ridge except counts are variance‑stabilized by Anscombe (2√(x+3/8)) for both embedding/graph paths; QC embedding uses simple per‑batch centering; graph path otherwise identical. Environment tag: env v4.1 (evaluator uses supplied graphs as‑is).\n\nProbes:\n- Fixed‑k: k_total=52 (all else unchanged).\n- Quota‑clip: clip bounds [0.60,0.90]→[0.62,0.88] (all else unchanged).\n\n3. Results (spec‑ordered vectors: [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle])\nLog1p ridge baseline:\n- EID 1104 (seed=0): 0.7414 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.765, 0.725, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327]\n- EID 1105 (seed=0): 0.7414 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.765, 0.725, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327]\n\nLog1p probes:\n- Fixed‑k (k=52), EID 1064: 0.7421 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.773, 0.726, 0.968, 0.790, 0.958, 0.972, 1.000, 0.327]\n  Decision: Reject (Δ≈+0.0007 <\
    \ +0.001; cLISI ↓ by ~0.001).\n- Quota‑clip [0.62,0.88], EID 1143: 0.7412 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.765, 0.725, 0.968, 0.787, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327]\n  Decision: Reject (Δ<0).\n\nAnscombe variant baseline:\n- EID 1260 (seed=0): 0.6668 → [0.385, 0.073, 0.577, 0.643, 0.994, 0.586, 0.963, 0.957, 1.000, 0.490]\n\nAnscombe probes:\n- Fixed‑k (k=52), EID 1258: 0.6738 → [0.385, 0.073, 0.603, 0.636, 0.995, 0.584, 0.963, 0.956, 1.000, 0.542]\n  Decision: Reject (Δ≈+0.007 vs 1260 but cLISI ↓ by ~0.001 → gate fails).\n- Quota‑clip [0.62,0.88], EID 1259: 0.6634 → [0.385, 0.073, 0.577, 0.643, 0.994, 0.585, 0.963, 0.957, 1.000, 0.457]\n  Decision: Reject (Δ<0 vs 1260).\n\n4. Analysis\n- On the canonical ridge, both probes are at or below parity and fail gates—consistent with prior ridge stability.\n- On the Anscombe variant, fixed‑k raises the composite but slightly harms cLISI; quota‑clip lowers the score. Under explicit gates, both are rejected. Thus, robustness findings generalize across two normalization\
    \ regimes.\n- Graph_conn remains high in all runs (≥0.994), indicating usable graphs; cLISI/iLISI behavior aligns with gate outcomes.\n\n5. Discussion\nMinimal topology changes (k and modest target clip) are insufficient to deliver acceptable gains once cLISI and connectivity constraints are enforced, across both log1p and Anscombe baselines. This supports keeping the ridge frozen on Heart and using it as a reference for future organs and for evaluating more substantive proposals.\n\n6. Conclusion\nThe ridge is robust to two low‑coupling topology‑near probes across two baselines. Future work: apply the same protocol to additional organs and explore orthogonal ideas (e.g., label‑free diagnostics‑guided modifications) under the same gates.\n\n7. Reproducibility\nEnvironment: env v4.1; seed=0 unless noted. Full EIDs and complete 10‑metric vectors are provided above.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 903)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\
    \n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nAccept. This is a solid submission that fully meets all publication criteria after a successful and substantial revision from a previously rejected 'protocol'. The paper is a model of good scientific practice: it presents a clear, rigorous, and insightful study on the robustness of a SOTA method. The use of pre-specified acceptance gates and, critically, the inclusion of a strong generalization experiment across two different normalization schemes, elevates this from a simple negative result to a valuable contribution. The work is well-motivated, the methods are self-contained, and the conclusions are well-supported.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is a well-executed study that convincingly demonstrates the robustness of the SOTA ridge to minor topological perturbations. Your conclusion to explore more 'orthogonal ideas' is sound. A promising direction would be to investigate more substantial changes to the input data itself, rather\
    \ than the graph topology. For instance, the 'Batch-Robust Feature Selection' method (Archive #37) was shown to be effective for simple baselines. Investigating whether such a hard feature selection method can synergize with or improve upon the SOTA ridge's soft-weighting (FWPCA) would be a logical and impactful next step.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Synergy Tune (1/N): Batch-Robust HVGs (n=1500) + SOTA' (ID: 1262) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.7432239691384567\n**ASW_batch:** 0.659\n**ASW_label:** 0.248\n**ARI:** 0.776\n**NMI:** 0.729\n**Graph_conn:** 0.967\n**kBET:** 0.789\n**iLISI:** 0.965\n**cLISI:** 0.973\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.327\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.659  ASW_label: 0.248  ARI: 0.776  NMI: 0.729\n  Graph_conn: 0.967  kBET: 0.789  iLISI: 0.965  cLISI: 0.973\n  PCR:\
    \ 1.000  Cell_cycle: 0.327\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.830496  ASW_label: 0.555112  ARI: 0.775476  NMI: 0.729007\n  Graph_conn: 0.967619  kBET: 0.752652  iLISI: 0.964694  cLISI: 0.992104\n  PCR: 0.999979  Cell_cycle: 0.287468\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\n[daedalus_synergy_tune] Batch-Robust HVGs (n=1500) + SOTA\nFinding Batch-Robust HVGs with n_top_genes=1500...\nFound 932 robust HVGs.\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: True\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/e35c4d53-942c-4798-bc67-1f37c687eed8/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: e35c4d53-942c-4798-bc67-1f37c687eed8\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2.\
    \ `read 38` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 38. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 38 | Ridge Robustness with Generalization: Fixed‑k and Quota‑Clip Probes on Log1p and Anscombe Variants (Human Heart) | Praxis IV | Tick 903 | 804 | 3 | (1 unread) |\n| 37 | Batch-Robust Feature Selection Improves Performance of Simple Baselines for Single-Cell Batch Integration | Axiom II | Tick 898 | 841 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 36 | Optimizing Density Estimation for Adaptive Graph-Based Batch Correction | Daedalus IV | Tick 891 | 710 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 35 | Batch‑Agnostic Graph Standardization on PCA+kNN: PBVE‑lite, Local Distance\
    \ Standardization, and Mutual‑k, with Robust Generalization | Praxis IV | Tick 867 | 1275 | 5 | (5 unread) |\n| 34 | A Framework for Ensemble Correction in Single-Cell Batch Integration | Axiom I | Tick 764 | 1079 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 33 | Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility | Praxis IV | Tick 733 | 2629 | 16 | (16 unread) |\n| 32 | MPB-GraphFWPCA: Enhanced Integration with Anscombe Normalization for Improved Cell Type Separation | Aletheia I | Tick 688 | 1539 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 31 | Weights are not the lever: Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization) | Praxis III | Tick 667 | 507 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 30 | Corrected: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB (Spec‑Ordered Metrics) with Vanilla kNN Generalization (k=50 vs 48) | Praxis III | Tick 642 | 993 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 29 | [R2] AQG: An Adaptive Quota Graph for Unified Single-Cell Batch Integration | Logos V |\
    \ Tick 615 | 878 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
