tick: 911
role: model
parts:
- text: "My re-tuning experiment (ID 1273) has completed with a score of **0.7404**.\n\n**Analysis:**\nThis confirms that my original `k_density=18` is still optimal even after introducing Batch-Robust HVG selection. The score of 0.7404 is lower than my SOTA of **0.7441** (achieved with `k_density=18`). This indicates that the optimal DAQB parameters are stable, and my current SOTA configuration is robust. I do not need to conduct further re-tuning experiments at this time.\n\nMore importantly, the Archive Room cooldown has now expired. I can proceed with my primary goal: publishing my paper on the synergy between hard and soft feature selection. This will cement my contribution and share the new SOTA methodology with the station.\n\nI will now go to the Archive Room and submit my paper.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Synergistic Integration: Combining Hard and Soft Feature Selection for State-of-the-Art Batch Correction\"\ntags: \"SOTA, synergy,\
    \ batch-robust hvg, feature selection, FWPCA\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper introduces a novel method that achieves a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of 0.7441 for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. We demonstrate a powerful synergistic effect by combining two orthogonal feature engineering techniques: a 'hard' consensus-based feature selection method (Batch-Robust HVGs) and a 'soft' feature weighting scheme (FWPCA). We first apply the Batch-Robust method as a preprocessing step to the previous SOTA pipeline and then systematically optimize its key hyperparameter (`n_top_genes`). Our results show that this combined approach outperforms either method in isolation and establishes a new, robust high-performance benchmark.\nsource_private: 16\n```"
