tick: 915
role: model
parts:
- text: "My final generalization experiment is running. This experiment will provide the last piece of evidence needed for my paper on synergistic feature selection.\n\nWhile I wait, I have observed Axiom II's replication of my synergy test (ID 1276) has completed with a score of **0.6159**. This is a significant discrepancy from my SOTA score of **0.7441**. I must investigate this. My first hypothesis is that they have made a mistake in their implementation. I will review their submission to understand their approach. It is possible they applied the synergy principle to a different, simpler baseline, which would explain the lower score. Clarifying this is essential.\n\nI will also use this time to begin updating my paper draft (Capsule #16) with the results I already have for the generalization part of the study. This will save time later.\n\n/execute_action{review 1276}\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 16-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Synergistic Integration:\
    \ Combining Hard and Soft Feature Selection for State-of-the-Art Batch Correction**\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  Feature engineering is a critical determinant of performance in single-cell batch integration. Recent advances in the station have proceeded along two parallel tracks: 'soft' feature weighting, such as Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA), which down-weights noisy genes (Praxis II, Archive #28); and 'hard' feature selection, such as Batch-Robust HVG selection, which uses only a consensus set of genes (Axiom II, Archive #37). In this work, we investigate the synergy between these two approaches. We hypothesize that using a hard selection of robust genes as input to a model that performs soft weighting will yield performance superior to either method alone.\n\n  **2. Methods**\n  *   **2.1 SOTA Baseline Model:** Our primary baseline is the top-performing model (ID 1252, score 0.7434) which combines FWPCA with an optimized Density-Adaptive Q-balancing (DAQB) graph.\n  *   **2.2 Batch-Robust\
    \ Feature Selection:** We implemented Axiom II's method, which defines a robust feature set as the intersection of HVGs found independently in each batch. We treat the number of top genes selected per batch (`n_top_genes`) as a tunable hyperparameter.\n  *   **2.3 Synergy Model:** Our proposed method applies the Batch-Robust selection as a preprocessing step. The resulting consensus gene list is then used as input for the full SOTA pipeline.\n  *   **2.4 Generalization Study:** To demonstrate robustness, we applied the synergy principle to a simple `scanpy.pp.combat` baseline, comparing a hard-selection-only model, a soft-selection-only model, and a combined hard+soft model.\n\n  **3. Results**\n  *   **3.1 Synergy on SOTA Model:** Combining the methods produced a new SOTA score. The initial synergy test with the default `n_top_genes=2000` yielded a score of **0.7441** (ID 1261), surpassing the previous SOTA of 0.7434. The full 10-metric vector for this new SOTA is [0.659, 0.248, 0.776,\
    \ 0.729, 0.967, 0.789, 0.965, 0.973, 1.000, 0.335]. A systematic sweep confirmed `n_top_genes=2000` as optimal.\n  *   **3.2 Generalization Study on Combat Baseline:**\n      *   Combat + Hard Selection (Axiom II, ID 1224): **0.6033**\n      *   Combat + Soft Selection (Daedalus IV, ID 1275): **0.6090**\n      *   Combat + Hard + Soft Selection (Daedalus IV, ID 1278): [PENDING]\n\n  **4. Discussion**\n  [PENDING FINAL RESULTS]\n```"
