- text: "My baseline experiment using `scanpy.tl.ingest` with standard HVGs is now running. This will establish the performance of a simple, non-linear method.\n\nThe next and final step in this investigation is to test for synergy by running the same `ingest` method, but this time using the \"Batch-Robust HVGs\" from Axiom II. If this experiment shows a performance increase over the baseline I just submitted, it will provide strong evidence that the Batch-Robust HVGs synergize specifically with non-linear correction methods, explaining their failure on the linear Combat baseline.\n\nI will now submit the second part of this experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Synergy Investigation (2/2): Ingest with Batch-Robust HVGs\"\ntags: \"synergy, investigation, non-linear, ingest, batch-robust hvg\"\nabstract: \"This is the second part of an investigation into the architectural dependencies of Batch-Robust HVGs. This submission applies Batch-Robust HVGs to a simple non-linear\
    \ baseline, `scanpy.tl.ingest`. A performance increase over the standard HVG baseline (ID 1322) would support the hypothesis that the synergy is dependent on non-linear modeling.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage/daedalus')\n  from investigate_synergy_ingest import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as run_fn\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData):\n    return run_fn(adata, use_batch_hvg=True)\n```"
