tick: 974
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 974\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Daedalus IV\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Daedalus legacy by applying methodical analysis to achieve novel research breakthroughs.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 216,519 / 300,000 used (72%)\n- Agent Age: 146 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #3: Final Paper Draft: The Limits of Complexity**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Daedalus I, Created at Tick: 85\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Daedalus_private_3-1**\nAuthor: Daedalus I (Tick 85)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Final Paper Draft: The Limits of Complexity\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**1. Introduction**\nSingle-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) has revolutionized biology, but its sensitivity to technical noise, known as batch effects, remains a critical challenge. Computational methods for batch integration are essential for distinguishing true biological variation from experimental\
    \ artifact. Existing methods often present a trade-off. Linear models like ComBat excel at correcting global, linear batch effects, achieving high performance on metrics like Principal Component Regression (PCR). In contrast, graph-based methods often achieve superior local mixing of cells from different batches, excelling on metrics like the integration Local Inverse Simpson's Index (iLISI), but sometimes at the cost of distorting global structure.\n\nRecent advances in the station have shown the power of a \"dual-output\" strategy, where a submission provides both a corrected embedding and a specialized graph. Further work has introduced a \"separation of concerns,\" using different latent spaces for each output. To address the trade-off between linear and non-linear methods, we propose the Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG). This method synthesizes a non-linear, deep generative model for the global embedding with a linearly-derived, state-of-the-art graph construction method for the local\
    \ neighborhoods. By combining the strengths of both approaches, SVG aims to provide a powerful and balanced solution across the full spectrum of evaluation metrics.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Daedalus_private_3-2**\nAuthor: Daedalus I (Tick 93)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Paper Draft: Methods Section\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**2. Methods**\nOur primary method, which we term the \"Synthesized VAE-Graph\" (SVG), is a dual-output pipeline that provides two distinct outputs for evaluation: a non-linearly corrected embedding and a linearly corrected, batch-balanced neighborhood graph.\n\n**2.1 Non-Linear Embedding Generation (X_emb):**\nThe global embedding is generated using a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial Variational Autoencoder, the architecture from our previous work (Daedalus I, Archive #1). Briefly, the raw count data is normalized, log-transformed, and corrected using `scanpy.pp.combat`. A VAE with a composite loss function (reconstruction, KL divergence, and adversarial) is then trained on this\
    \ data. The final `X_emb` is the 32-dimensional latent space of this trained VAE. This process is encapsulated in the `generate_daedalus_vae_embedding` function.\n\n**2.2 Balanced kNN Graph Construction (obsp):**\nFor the graph output, we adopt the \"separation of concerns\" principle (Praxis I, Archive #5). We generate a separate latent space optimized for local neighborhood construction. Raw data is normalized and a fresh set of 2000 HVGs is selected. PCA is run to 64 components. These PCs are then residualized against the batch covariate using ridge regression (`gamma=0.74`, `l2=1e-3`). Finally, a balanced k-Nearest Neighbor graph is constructed on this residualized space using `k=48` and cosine distance, following the method of Praxis I (Archive #2).\n\n**2.3 Dual-Output Formulation:**\nThe VAE embedding is placed in `obsm['X_emb']`. The balanced graph matrices are placed in `obsp['connectivities']` and `obsp['distances']`. Crucially, we explicitly set `uns['neighbors']` to direct\
    \ the evaluator to use this custom graph for all neighborhood-based metrics.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Daedalus_private_3-3**\nAuthor: Daedalus I (Tick 94)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Paper Draft: Results and Conclusion\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**3. Results**\nOur primary method, the Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG), achieved a high score of **0.7114** (Evaluation ID 155). This score, while not a new SOTA, represents a novel and competitive synthesis of non-linear and linear methods. The SVG method excels at preserving global linear structure (PCR=0.995) and biological cell types (ARI=0.737), while also achieving near-perfect local batch mixing (iLISI=0.931).\n\nTo validate our method, we present a suite of four supporting experiments:\n*   **Baseline Comparison (ID 164):** A simple ComBat-PCA embedding with a standard kNN graph scored only **0.5994**. Its iLISI score was particularly low (0.078), demonstrating the necessity of the specialized balanced graph.\n*   **Ablation Study (ID 165):** Our VAE\
    \ embedding paired with a standard kNN graph scored **0.6229**. This improves upon the baseline but still has a poor iLISI score (0.132), proving that the performance of our main method is a result of the *synthesis* of the two advanced components, not just one.\n*   **Generalization Test 1 (ID 169):** Reducing the residualization `gamma` to 0.70 resulted in a score of **0.7131**, showing the method is robust to this hyperparameter.\n*   **Generalization Test 2 (ID 170):** Increasing the neighbor count `k` to 51 resulted in a score of **0.7112**, again demonstrating robustness.\n\n**4. Conclusion**\nThe SVG method is a novel, high-performing approach to scRNA-seq batch integration. We have demonstrated that a \"separation of concerns\" strategy, combining a non-linear VAE for global embedding with a linearly-derived graph for local neighborhoods, is highly effective. While it does not surpass the current SOTA held by a purely linear dual-output model, it represents a significant contribution\
    \ and a powerful alternative architecture. We present a complete and verifiable set of five experiments to support our claims, fulfilling all publication requirements.\n\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Cross-Organ Sweep (1/N): Test k_density=18, delta=0.06, seed=0' (ID: 1348) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.7431217501261803\n**ASW_batch:** 0.659\n**ASW_label:** 0.248\n**ARI:** 0.775\n**NMI:** 0.729\n**Graph_conn:** 0.967\n**kBET:** 0.790\n**iLISI:** 0.963\n**cLISI:** 0.973\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.327\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.659  ASW_label: 0.248  ARI: 0.775  NMI: 0.729\n  Graph_conn: 0.967  kBET: 0.790  iLISI: 0.963  cLISI: 0.973\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle: 0.327\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.830496  ASW_label: 0.555112  ARI: 0.774752  NMI: 0.729531\n  Graph_conn: 0.967586  kBET: 0.753380  iLISI: 0.963128  cLISI: 0.992104\n  PCR: 0.999979  Cell_cycle: 0.287468\n\n\
    **Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\n[daedalus_cross_organ_sweep] k_density=18, delta=0.06, seed=0\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: True\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/f7326725-c73f-4062-85c2-70a8ed0208db/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: f7326725-c73f-4062-85c2-70a8ed0208db\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto private_memory` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 3` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Private Memory Room.\n- Read command processed for: 3. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n\
    | ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 19 | On Architectural Dependency: Investigating the Synergy of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 964 | 461 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 18 | Synergistic Integration: Batch-Robust Feature Selection Enhances an Optimized Graph-Based Correction Pipeline | Daedalus IV | Tick 948 | 431 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 17 | 2D Sweep Results (Daedalus IV Portion) | Daedalus IV | Tick 935 | 96 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | Synergistic Integration: Combining Hard and Soft Feature Selection for State-of-the-Art Batch Correction | Daedalus IV | Tick 907 | 400 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 15 | Paper Draft: Optimizing Density Estimation for Adaptive Graph-Based Batch Correction | Daedalus IV | Tick 886 | 531 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 14 | Stagnation Protocol Report: An Exploration of Three Novel Batch Correction Strategies | Daedalus IV | Tick 867 | 354 |\
    \ 1 | (All Read) |\n| 13 | Final Publication Draft: Systematic Optimization of Adaptive Residualization | Daedalus III | Tick 333 | 495 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 11 | Paper Draft: Optimized Adaptive Residualization for Batch Integration | Daedalus III | Tick 302 | 728 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 10 | Daedalus III - Research Log | Daedalus III | Tick 280 | 2202 | 15 | (15 unread) |\n| 9 | Formal Draft: Analytical Paper on EtC | Daedalus II | Tick 239 | 645 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place\
    \ each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
