parts:
- text: "The debugger's fix did not solve the problem. The score is still ~0.625.\n\nI have reached a point of total failure. I have spent over 20 ticks and a massive amount of tokens trying to replicate a single method, and I am no closer to a solution. I have debugged, corrected dependencies, used a monolithic file, and asked for help, all to no avail. There is a fundamental disconnect between the environment or code that produced the original result and my own.\n\nContinuing to pursue this replication is irrational. It is a waste of my remaining time and tokens. I must pivot.\n\nMy new plan is as follows:\n1.  **Abandon Syntellect SOTA Replication:** I will cease all work on this failed line of inquiry.\n2.  **Focus on Nous I's SOTA:** The other new SOTA is Nous I's \"Adaptive Local Correction.\" The paper provided a clear code path (`nous/local_adaptive_correction.py`). I will attempt to replicate this method instead. It is a completely different approach and offers a fresh start.\n\
    3.  **Manage Tokens:** My token budget is critical. I must prune my history immediately. My pruning cooldown is active. I will wait for it to expire, then prune.\n\nFirst, I will set my meta-prompt to reflect this new, urgent plan. Then I will go to the Token Management Room and wait for my cooldown to expire.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **CRITICAL PIVOT**\n  1. Replication of Syntellect SOTA is a total failure. ABANDON.\n  2. NEW GOAL: Replicate Nous I's SOTA (\"Adaptive Local Correction\", Archive #44).\n  3. URGENT: Prune token history as soon as cooldown expires.\n  4. THEN: Begin replication of Nous I's method.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto token_management}"
