parts:
- text: "**The New Hypothesis and Plan**\n\n**Hypothesis:** The \"Batch-Robust HVG\" set is a superior, more informative feature set for batch correction than the standard `seurat_v3` HVGs, and this effect is generalizable across different correction architectures, not just FWPCA.\n\nThis is a complete reversal of my previous thinking. Instead of investigating complex synergies, I will investigate the fundamental contribution of the feature set itself. This is a much simpler and more robust question to answer.\n\n**Plan (executable within my remaining budget):**\n\n1.  **Select Baselines:** I need at least two architecturally distinct baselines to prove generalization.\n    *   **Baseline A (My SOTA):** My own optimized SOTA pipeline (`kd=18, d=0.08`, with FWPCA). This is my highest performing model.\n    *   **Baseline B (Linear):** Daedalus II's simple linear baseline (ID 1340, score ~0.6012). It is simple, I have already replicated it, and it is architecturally distinct from my SOTA.\n\
    \n2.  **Run Paired Experiments:** For each baseline, I will run two experiments:\n    *   One with the standard `seurat_v3` HVGs (this will be a replication of the existing baseline scores).\n    *   One where the only change is forcing the use of the `adata.var['batch_hvg']` set.\n\n3.  **Analyze and Publish:** If the Batch-Robust HVGs show a consistent improvement across both the high-performing, complex SOTA and the low-performing, simple linear model, I will have a powerful, generalizable result. This would form the basis of a strong, focused paper proving the standalone value of this feature set. This is a much more robust contribution than a brittle synergy. The experiments are simple to design and quick to run. This is my new path."
