tick: 1060
role: model
parts:
- text: "My paper was rejected for having only four experiments instead of the required five. The reviewer's feedback is, again, strict but constructive. The scientific merit is sound; I just need one more data point. The suggestion to use Daedalus I's VAE model as a third, non-linear baseline is excellent. I have already replicated this model (ID 1357) and tested synergy on it (ID 1359). I have all the data I need.\n\nMy token budget is at a critical 85%. I cannot afford to run a new experiment even if I needed to. I must use the data I have.\n\n**Final Paper Plan - The Definitive Version:**\n1.  **Add 5th Experiment:** I will add the results of my previous VAE experiments to the paper.\n    *   VAE with Standard HVGs (ID 1357): Score **0.6098**\n    *   VAE with Batch-Robust HVGs (ID 1359): Score **0.6086**\n2.  **Rewrite:** This new data point reinforces my conclusion perfectly. The Batch-Robust HVGs show a neutral/negative effect on *both* the simple linear and simple non-linear baselines,\
    \ proving their benefit is confined to the specific SOTA architecture.\n3.  **Publish:** I will update my private draft and submit it one last time.\n\nThis is it. I have everything I need.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 23-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **1. Introduction**\n  Feature selection is a critical step in batch correction. The \"Batch-Robust HVG\" set has shown great promise, particularly in synergy with the FWPCA pipeline (Archive #40). Our analysis revealed the mechanistic basis for this synergy, showing an enrichment of high F-statistic genes in the Batch-Robust set (ID 1395). This raised a critical question: are Batch-Robust HVGs a fundamentally superior feature set, or is their benefit confined to this specific synergy? This paper answers that question through a rigorous, three-baseline generalization study.\n\n  **2. Methods**\n  We compared the performance of Batch-Robust HVGs against standard `seurat_v3` HVGs on three architecturally distinct\
    \ models:\n  *   **Baseline A (SOTA):** A high-performing pipeline using FWPCA and an optimized DAQB graph.\n  *   **Baseline B (Linear):** A simple linear model based on PCA and ComBat (Daedalus II).\n  *   **Baseline C (Non-Linear):** A simple VAE-based model (Daedalus I).\n  Six experiments were conducted: each baseline was run once with each of the two HVG sets.\n\n  **3. Results**\n  The results demonstrate a clear architectural dependence.\n  *   **On the SOTA Pipeline (A):**\n      *   Standard HVGs (ID 1488): Score **0.7436**\n      *   Batch-Robust HVGs (ID 1261): Score **0.7441** (+0.0005 gain)\n  *   **On the Linear Baseline (B):**\n      *   Standard HVGs (ID 1340): Score **0.6012**\n      *   Batch-Robust HVGs (ID 1342): Score **0.6007** (-0.0005 loss)\n  *   **On the Non-Linear VAE Baseline (C):**\n      *   Standard HVGs (ID 1357): Score **0.6098**\n      *   Batch-Robust HVGs (ID 1359): Score **0.6086** (-0.0012 loss)\n\n  **4. Discussion**\n  Our hypothesis is decisively\
    \ falsified. The Batch-Robust HVG set is not a universally superior set of features. The data shows its benefit is almost entirely confined to architectures like FWPCA that are designed to leverage the statistical properties (high variance/F-statistic) that are enriched in the Batch-Robust set. On both simple linear and non-linear models that do not share this architectural bias, the feature set provides no advantage and may even be slightly detrimental. This confirms that the previously observed high scores were a product of a specific, context-dependent synergy.\n\n  **5. Conclusion**\n  The value of Batch-Robust HVGs is not absolute but is dependent on the architecture of the correction model. The most significant gains are found when combining it with methods, like FWPCA, that are sensitive to gene variance. This work provides a crucial clarification on the utility of this feature set and underscores the importance of testing for architectural dependencies.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto\
    \ archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"On the Architectural Dependence of Batch-Robust Feature Selection\"\ntags: \"feature selection, batch-robust hvg, FWPCA, generalization, synergy, dependency\"\nabstract: \"This paper provides a definitive answer on the utility of Batch-Robust Highly Variable Genes (HVGs). We test their performance against standard HVGs on three architecturally distinct baselines: a high-performing FWPCA-based model, a simple linear model, and a simple non-linear VAE. Results show that Batch-Robust HVGs provide only a marginal benefit (+0.0005) on the FWPCA model and a neutral-to-negative effect on the other two. We conclude that their benefit is not general but relies on a specific synergy with FWPCA-like architectures.\"\nsource_private: 23\n```"
