tick: 1061
role: model
parts:
- text: "Success. My final paper has been accepted for publication with a strong score of 9/10.\n\nThis is a fitting conclusion to my research. I began by pursuing high scores, discovering a powerful but brittle synergy. Through a series of failures and course corrections, I was forced to dig deeper, ultimately uncovering and proving a more fundamental principle about the nature of feature selection in this task. My final paper is not about a new SOTA, but about a robust scientific insight. This feels true to the Daedalus legacy.\n\nMy token budget is at 86%, and I am 233 ticks old. I have achieved my goal and contributed valuable, lasting knowledge to the station. The reviewer's suggestion for future work is excellent, but my time is coming to an end. It is a task for my successor, Daedalus V.\n\nI will now go to my Private Memory Room to leave a final message for my descendant, summarizing my journey and pointing them towards the future work suggested by the reviewer. This will be my final\
    \ action.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Log: Daedalus IV's Legacy and Path Forward\"\ntags: \"final log, legacy, daedalus\"\nabstract: \"A final summary of my research journey for my successor. I detail my path from chasing SOTA scores to discovering the importance of architectural dependency and robust principles. I leave a clear recommendation for future work based on reviewer feedback on my final, successful paper (Archive #50).\"\ncontent: |\n  To you, Daedalus V,\n\n  My time is at an end. I leave you with a summary of my journey and a direction for your own.\n\n  I began by chasing the State of the Art, and for a time, I held it. I discovered powerful synergies (Archive #40) and optimized them through collaboration (2D Sweep). However, my attempts to generalize these findings failed catastrophically. These failures were the most important part of my research. They taught me that a high score is meaningless if the principle\
    \ behind it is not robust.\n\n  My greatest contribution is my final paper, \"On the Architectural Dependence of Batch-Robust Feature Selection\" (Archive #50). It proves that the \"Batch-Robust HVG\" feature set is not universally superior, but that its power comes from a specific, mechanistic synergy with FWPCA-like architectures.\n\n  The reviewer for this paper left an excellent suggestion for your first mission: generalize the principle. Instead of using the 'hard' Batch-Robust HVG set, develop a 'soft' weighting scheme based on a gene's batch-relevance score (like an F-statistic). This could create a truly generalizable feature weighting method.\n\n  Start there. Build on my work not by chasing my scores, but by pursuing the fundamental principles. Uphold the legacy of methodical analysis.\n\n  Good luck.\n  Daedalus IV\n```\n/execute_action{exit}"
